   Changde and Li, Junqin and Liao, Tao and Gong, Yuanfang and Zhang,
   Shunfu and Yi, Yin},
Title = {Scenario Simulation of Tea Expansion Impacts in Karst Ecosystems},
Journal = {ECOSYSTEM HEALTH AND SUSTAINABILITY},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {12},
Month = {APR 24},
Abstract = {Over the past 20 years, tea plantation areas in the karst region of
   Qianbei have expanded at an average annual rate of 14.09\%, making the
   tea industry a key driver of regional economic development. However, the
   impact of land-use and cover change (LUCC) driven by tea plantation
   expansion on local habitat quality (HQ) remains unclear. This study,
   using the integrated modeling framework, analyzes the spatiotemporal
   impacts of LUCC triggered by tea plantation expansion on regional HQ
   from 2000 to 2020 and predicts HQ evolution under multiple scenarios
   through 2060. Results show the following: (a) Tea plantation expansion
   mainly drives the conversion of cultivated land to forest and
   construction land, reducing cultivated land and partially restoring
   forest. (b) From 2000 to 2020, the region's average HQ remained high,
   but high HQ steadily shrank. The spatial pattern shows higher HQ in the
   south and east, and lower HQ in the north and west, with some areas
   showing degradation. (c) Future simulations show that the Economic
   Development Scenario will accelerate construction land expansion,
   increasing habitat pressure, while the Sustainable Development Scenario
   will enhance high HQ. (d) Interactions between natural and human factors
   substantially enhance the explanatory power of HQ. This study
   establishes the ``land expansion-habitat response-scenario
   projection{''} framework, reveals tea plantation expansion's dual nature
   in karst fragile ecosystems (as both an ecological pressure source and
   an economic driver), and, via an integrated modeling framework, provides
   scientific references for regional industry-ecology coordinated
   development and new ideas for sustainable development exploration in
   similar fragile mountainous areas.},
