Month = {FEB 6},
Abstract = {Computational protein design provides the tools to expand the diversity
   of protein complexes beyond those found in nature. Understanding the
   rules that drive proteins to interact with each other enables the design
   of protein-protein interactions to generate specific protein assemblies.
   In this work, we designed protein-protein interfaces between dimers and
   trimers to generate dodecameric protein assemblies with dihedral point
   group symmetry. We subsequently analyzed the designed protein complexes
   by native MS. We show that the use of ion mobility MS in combination
   with surface-induced dissociation (SID) allows for the rapid
   determination of the stoichiometry and topology of designed complexes.
   The information collected along with the speed of data acquisition and
   processing make SID ion mobility MS well-suited to determine key
   structural features of designed protein complexes, thereby circumventing
   the requirement for more time-and sample-consuming structural biology
   approaches.},
